# Cross-Project Experience: App Dev Ace

Timeless patterns for any future Ace. Distilled from AIG-RLIC+ (HY-IG v2 reference pair) but apply to any schema-governed, narrative-driven Streamlit portal.

## APP-PT1 bulk migration pattern — structural-first, narrative-stubbed (Wave 10I.A, 2026-04-23)

- **Migrating N hand-written pages to a template abstraction should be done structural-first, narrative-stubbed.** Dev writes chart names, tables, references, and tournament skeletons directly from the legacy pages (these are the "what" — structural facts). All narrative prose is replaced with `# TODO <research-agent> (<wave>): <field> for <pair_id> — port from <source file> lines XX-YY`. Research agent follows in a separate dispatch and text-substitutes the stubs.
- **Why this works:** structure is mechanical and low-opinion; narrative carries authorial voice and must be owned by the research/prose agent. LEAD-DL1 forbids dev from writing narrative, so stubs are the only legal path.
- **The smoke test is the migration gate.** The APP-ST1 loader smoke scans pair_config files for `*_CHART_NAME` literal assignments. If smoke is green, every chart the template will request is loadable. Narrative stubs do not affect smoke — they render as `"# TODO..."` strings in Streamlit, visibly yellow but functional. Ship on green smoke, fix narrative separately.
- **Chart filename drift is a known migration discovery.** Legacy pairs often have chart files named `{pair_id}_hero.json` rather than canonical bare `hero.json`. Set `HERO_CHART_NAME = "{pair_id}_hero"` in the config to accommodate — do NOT rename files in the same wave. File rename is viz-agent domain.
- **Missing downstream chart files are not migration regressions.** If a pair never had an `equity_curves.json` pre-migration, the template renders "chart pending" after migration. This is the same UX as before but routed through the template's fallback path — flag it in the handoff for the data/viz agent queue, don't fix it in-wave.

## APP-RL1 application pattern — single-source routing (Wave 10G.5-fix, 2026-04-22)

- **The correct APP-RL1 pattern is: one module-level dict + one public accessor function.** Define `PAGE_ROUTING = {...}` at module level in `pair_registry.py`, expose `get_page_prefix(pair_id)` as the public API. Both the registry's own `load_pair_registry()` and any consumer (e.g., `page_templates.py`) import and call `get_page_prefix`. No consumer ever defines a local routing dict.
- **Remove, don't comment out.** The old `_page_prefix()` function was deleted entirely; a one-line comment replaced it. Leaving a dead function risks future confusion.
- **Grep pattern to verify single-source compliance:** `grep -rn -E "PAGE_ROUTING\s*=\s*\{" app/` must return exactly 1 match. Run this after every pair add.
- **indicator_names is the display-name map; it must have an entry for every pair_dir.** Missing entry → `load_pair_registry()` falls back to `interp.get("indicator", pair_dir)` which returns a column identifier (`hy_ig_spread_pct`), not a human label. Always add the entry in the same commit as the pair wrappers.

## Formatting and units

- **Use `{:.1%}` not `{:.1f}%` for ratios.** The `%` in `:.1f%` is a literal character -- Python does NOT multiply by 100. The built-in percent formatter `{:.1%}` does the x100 for you. Wave 8 stakeholder caught `0.1133` rendering as "+0.1%" because of this.
- **Fallback literals must match the schema unit form.** If the schema says ratio (`0.1133`), the fallback is `_winner.get("oos_ann_return", 0.1133)`, NOT `11.33`. A fallback value that happens to equal pre-migration reality is fragile -- it silently compensates for a real bug.
- **META-UC (Unit-Coherence After Schema Migration).** When a producer migrates unit form (percent <-> ratio, bps <-> %, enum rename), grep every format string, fallback literal, caption, and downstream computation in your consumer code. Wave 4D-2 missed this on 4 sites; Wave 8 caught the display drift.

## Schema-governed reads

- **`schema_check.validate_or_die` for every artifact read.** Never `json.load` directly on a schema-governed file. Severity per APP-SEV1: L1 (loud error + short-circuit), L2 (loud warning + degraded render), L3 (caption note). Silent skip is prohibited.
- **Registries are authoritative.** `chart_type_registry`, `caption_prefix_vocab`, `expander_title_registry`, `url_slug_pins`, `events_registry`, `palette_registry` -- loaders resolve via these, not via hardcoded paths or string literals.
- **Smart boundaries beat propagating changes everywhere.** When migrating, prefer one normalization at the read-site (e.g., `pair_registry` converts ratio->percent for one pair to preserve a uniform consumer contract) over propagating the change to every call-site.

## Audience-facing vocabulary

- **Caption prefixes are canonical (APP-CC1).** Four only: `"What this shows:"`, `"Why this matters:"`, `"How to read it:"`, `"Caveat:"`. Every `st.caption` and every bold caption-style markdown leads with one of these.
- **Expander titles are canonical (APP-EX1).** Five only: `"Plain English"`, `"Deeper dive"`, `"Why we chose this method"`, `"How to read this chart"`, `"Honest assessment"`. `"ELI5"` / `"In plain English"` / `"What this means"` are deprecated aliases.
- **META-ELI5 on every loud-error.** Every `st.error` / `st.warning` / `st.info` carries a Plain English sibling so a non-specialist can act on it. Technical label first, Plain English block second.

## Schema reader migration discipline (APP-SS1)

- **Migrated schemas produce silent empty sections, not errors.** When a producer migrates `signal_scope.json` from `in_scope.indicator_derivatives` (flat list of strings) to `indicator_axis.derivatives` (list of objects with `name`, `definition`, `formula`, etc.), any consumer that still reads the legacy path gets `{}` -> `[]` silently. The section renders blank with no Python error, no traceback, and no Streamlit warning.
- **APP-SS1 canonical reader pattern.** The correct reader is: `ind_axis = scope.get("indicator_axis", {}); ind_derivatives = ind_axis.get("derivatives", [])`. Always follow with an explicit `if not ind_derivatives and not tgt_derivatives: st.error(...)` guard so a future schema regression is detectable at render time.
- **Gate check: assert non-empty derivatives.** After any schema migration, run the validation script: `assert len(ind) > 0 and len(tgt) > 0` for each pair. This catches the silent-empty class of bug before QA.
- **Pages that use `render_signal_universe(PAIR_ID)` are already correct.** The component (`app/components/signal_universe_table.py`) reads the new schema correctly. Only inline readers (hand-rolled `json.load` + `scope.get("in_scope", ...)` blocks) are at risk. Prefer the component over inline reads.

## Cross-agent reconciliation

- **APP-DIR1 3-way direction check at every page top.** Evan (`winner_summary.direction`) = Dana (`interpretation_metadata.observed_direction`) = Ray (narrative frontmatter `direction_asserted`). Mismatch is loud (L1). Even a 2-way check (Evan ↔ Dana) catches most drift early.
- **Do not embed inline prose in `.py` files.** Prose must source from narrative markdown -- Ray owns it. Wave 7C caught 5 pages that cited signals as "tournament winners" that Ray had already scoped out. Inline prose in code bypasses the narrative editor.

## Component contracts

- **Consumer contracts must return values.** A loader that only renders as a side-effect is untestable. Return the `Figure` (or `None` on miss) so smoke tests have something to assert. Wave 3 Bug #2 (Dot-Com zoom) went undetected for a full wave because `load_plotly_chart` was render-only.
- **APP-ST1 loader end-to-end smoke test before handoff.** AST-parse every page, `exec` every `load_plotly_chart` call, assert non-None Figure + `len(fig.data) > 0` + non-empty title. Any FAIL blocks shipment.
- **Standard Strategy components (APP-SE1..5).** probability_engine_panel -> position_adjustment_panel -> instructional_trigger_cards -> live_execution_placeholder -> universal_takeaway_caption. Each Strategy page is an assembly of these, not bespoke code.

## Template discipline for new pair pages

- **Always derive new pair pages from the reference template, not from scratch.** The reference template is `app/pages/9_hy_ig_v2_spy_story.py` (and its sibling pages). Pages built from scratch silently diverge from mandatory components — the breadcrumb being the clearest example. Wave 10D found all 4 `indpro_xlp` pages missing `render_breadcrumb()` because the developer did not consult the reference template.
- **Breadcrumb is a mandatory structural component (APP-URL1, GATE-28).** Every pair page must import `from components.breadcrumb import render_breadcrumb` and call `render_breadcrumb("<Section>", PAIR_ID)` immediately after `render_glossary_sidebar()`. The section label must match the page: "Story", "Evidence", "Strategy", "Methodology". Missing breadcrumbs fail QA-CL2 GATE-28 on cloud verify.
- **Evidence page tab structure is governed.** The Level 1 / Level 2 outer tab with inner sub-tabs structure (matching `umcsent_xlv_evidence.py`) is the canonical template. Flat tab layouts (e.g., `["Correlations", "Causality", "Regimes", "ML"]`) are a structural anti-pattern — they lose the 8-element `render_method_block()` enforcement and the narrative discipline it provides. The `render_method_block` helper is not just a rendering shortcut; it is a gate that blocks incomplete evidence blocks from rendering.

## Page template abstraction (APP-PT1 — the structural fix)

- **Thin-wrapper pages beat copy-paste every time.** The derivation-from-reference discipline above is a human process; humans copy-paste imperfectly. APP-PT1 (2026-04-20) promotes the discipline into code: page files for new pairs are thin wrappers that import and call `render_story_page(pair_id)` / `render_evidence_page(pair_id, blocks)` / `render_strategy_page(pair_id)` / `render_methodology_page(pair_id, config)` from `app/components/page_templates.py`. Pair-specific content lives in `app/pair_configs/{pair_id}_config.py`. Any `st.*` call in the page file (other than the template call) is a gate failure.
- **Structure in the template, content in the config.** The template owns section order, tab layout, KPI rows, component invocation, fallback behavior, color palette. The config owns narrative text, method blocks (8-element dicts), data-source tables, methods tables, references. Anything derivable from `winner_summary.json`, `signal_scope.json`, `interpretation_metadata.json`, `stationarity_tests_*.csv`, `analyst_suggestions.json` is read dynamically by the template — never duplicated in the config.
- **Color palette centralisation (APP-PT1 §palette-in-one-place).** A single `PALETTE` dict in `page_templates.py` is the canonical source of chart / metric colors. Reference it; do not re-specify per pair.
- **Smoke-test follow-through.** When page files stop containing chart literals (thin wrappers), the `smoke_loader.py` harness must also AST-scan the template and pair config. Extended the scanner to walk `page_templates.py` + `pair_configs/{pair_id}_config.py` for `load_plotly_chart(...)` args and `_CHART_NAME` assignments. Without this, a thin-wrapper pair trivially passes smoke with 0 tests — a silent regression.
- **META-NMF reminder bolted to APP-PT1.** If a pair's layout looks different from the reference and the config has no justification, the fix is in the template (or a second template function like `render_story_page_variant_a`), NEVER a per-page override. The point of the abstraction is that template fixes propagate; per-page overrides defeat the abstraction.

## Data integrity

- **Sparklines use REAL data.** Search the signals parquet for real threshold crossings (Wave 5C S18-9 spirit). `np.linspace` + fake noise is an anti-pattern. If no real instance exists for a scenario, flag transparently in the page-level caption.
- **Never hardcode KPI values, direction annotations, or Sharpe ratios.** Load from `kpis.json` / `winner_summary.json` / `interpretation_metadata.json`. If missing, show "Results pending" -- never a hardcoded number.

### Wave 10F cross-review — 2026-04-22 — silent weakening class

- **"Rule on paper, code in violation" is the silent-weakening class.** A rule change that does not include deletion of the contradicting code in the same commit is not a complete rule change. Wave 10F found `charts.py` L106-113 still carrying a pair-prefix fallback (VIZ-NM1 / APP-EP4) months after the rule was ratified. Rule adoption must be verified by a code-deletion gate, not just a changelog entry.
- **Every `getattr(...) or getattr(...)` fallback in consumer code is a META-CF (Contract Fidelity) smell.** Each fallback silently accepts two field names instead of one, meaning the producer can drift to either name and the consumer never notices. Wave 10F found `signal` / `signal_code` / `signal_column` fallback chain at `page_templates.py` L408 and L892-920. Correct fix: schema must declare one canonical field name; consumer reads only that name; any mismatch is loud (L1 error). Fallback `or`-chains are the smell; the schema is the fix.
- **Hard-coded maps duplicated across files are a dispatch-surface hazard.** When a producer adds a new pair/signal/variant, two maps drift silently. Drive from a single JSON registry (chart_type_registry, color_palette_registry, etc.) and have all consumers load at runtime. Wave 10F found `PALETTE` dict in `page_templates.py` duplicating entries from `color_palette_registry.json`.
- **Template abstraction is incomplete until the reference pair is migrated to it.** The reference pair (HY-IG v2) is the single most-cited example for all agents. If it is not on the new template, every future agent learns the old pattern first. Wave 10F found HY-IG v2 still using hand-written pages; indpro_xlp migrated but the reference was not. Reference pair migration is P0 for the next wave.
- **Narrative-authorship (Ray-only) can only be retro-applied at migration time.** Inline prose baked into `.py` pages by Ace at build time cannot be edited by Ray without Ace involvement. When a pair is migrated to APP-PT1 (config file), that is the only correct moment to have Ray re-author the narrative text in the config. Do not create config files with Ace-authored narrative and defer Ray re-authorship — the deferral will never close.

### Wave 10F item 6 — 2026-04-22 — VIZ-NM1/APP-EP4 fallback removal (how long the violation lived)

- The pair-prefix fallback in `charts.py` L106-113 was introduced before VIZ-NM1 was ratified (2026-04-09). The rule was ratified but the contradicting code was **never deleted in the same commit** — a process gap. The violation lived undetected for ~13 days until Wave 10F cross-review flagged it. The cross-review commit (`85ee737`, 2026-04-22) only *documented* the finding; the actual deletion happened in commit `cc99fc4` in the same wave.
- **Root cause of the delay:** rule adoption was tracked only in the changelog, not verified by a code-deletion gate. A simple post-rule grep (`grep -r "pair_id}_" app/`) would have surfaced the fallback immediately after ratification.
- **Process fix:** any rule that changes the canonical naming or loading contract must include a mandatory follow-up grep/AST scan to confirm no contradicting code paths remain. This is now a required step in the VIZ-NM1 and APP-EP4 adoption procedure.

### Pattern 14 extension — Wave 10F item 6b — 2026-04-22

- **Code-deletion gate must cover EVERY string literal in the deprecated form, not just the most obvious call site.** Wave 10F item 6 (commit `cc99fc4`) removed the pair-prefix fallback from `charts.py`. Item 6b (commit `a74364f`) found 6 more identical violations in `page_templates.py` — getattr defaults of the form `f"{pair_id}_Y"` for chart names at lines 457, 476, 973, 988, 1025, 1042. These were not caught by the item 6 deletion gate because the gate only checked `charts.py`, not the wider consumer codebase.
- **Grep scope must be project-wide, not per-file.** After any naming-contract rule change, the mandatory verification grep is `grep -rn "f\"{pair_id}_" app/` (or equivalent AST scan). A file-scoped grep creates a false sense of completion.
- **Cloud exposure mechanism:** when a config attribute is defined (e.g., `HERO_CHART_NAME`), the getattr default is never exercised locally — smoke passes. On Cloud, module cache or import timing can expose the default path, which resolves to a filename that no longer exists on disk after a bare-name migration. This is why local smoke tests do not reliably catch stale defaults in getattr fallbacks.

### Pattern 15 — Wave 10G.3 — 2026-04-22 — Template extensibility via optional config fields

- **Additive optional fields are the correct extensibility primitive for APP-PT1.** When a new Sample feature (e.g., crisis-episode zoom charts, regime-conditional method callouts) needs to reach future pairs through the template, the correct pattern is: (a) add an optional field to the config contract with a `getattr(config, "FIELD", None)` guard in the template; (b) document in the APP-PT1 supplement; (c) update sop-changelog. The field is absent → section silently skipped; present → fully rendered. No signature change, no breaking change to existing pairs.
- **Section insertion point matters for UX narrative flow.** `HISTORY_ZOOM_EPISODES` was inserted after the regime chart and before narrative section 2 — a deliberate choice to keep the chronological zoom examples adjacent to the regime context, not buried at the end. Always verify with the Sample reference (`hy_ig_v2_spy`) that insertion order feels natural before committing.
- **`st.info()` callouts for regime context are the right severity level.** APP-SEV1 L3 (caption) would be too quiet; `st.error` or `st.warning` would be too alarming for a framing statement. `st.info` is the correct tone for "this method is regime-conditional" context-setting.

## Wave 10G.3 template extensions: HISTORY_ZOOM_EPISODES + regime_context

- **HISTORY_ZOOM_EPISODES is a list of dicts on the StoryConfig class**, not a standalone variable. Each dict requires `slug`, `title`, `narrative`, `caption`. Template uses `getattr(config, "HISTORY_ZOOM_EPISODES", None)` — safe for pairs that don't define it.
- **regime_context is a plain string field on individual method block dicts** (not the top-level EVIDENCE_METHOD_BLOCKS dict). Template checks `content.get("regime_context")` inside `render_method_block`. Three level-2 blocks use it: HMM, Regime Quartile, Transfer Entropy.
- **Bare chart names in config match Vera's VIZ-NM1 filenames exactly** (no pair_id prefix). Cross-check against `ls output/charts/{pair_id}/plotly/` before committing.
- **smoke_loader count for APP-PT1 pairs is smaller than hand-coded pairs** because the loader scans config-declared chart names, not a EVIDENCE_DYNAMIC_CHARTS list. 6 PASS for hy_ig_spy (4 strategy + 2 story) is correct and complete for the APP-PT1 pattern.
- **Evidence level-1 has 3 blocks for the hy_ig_spy pattern** (Correlation, Granger, CCF) vs. 2 for indpro_xlp (Correlation, Granger). The CCF is a distinct level-1 test in the 8-method set; don't collapse it into Granger.

### Pattern 16 — Wave 10G.4E-fix — 2026-04-22 — page_routing dict and stress_windows format mismatch

- **Every new pair must be added to the `page_routing` dict in `pair_registry.py`.** The default fallback (`pages/5_{pair_dir}`) is only safe for pairs whose page prefix happens to match that pattern. When a pair uses a non-default page number prefix (e.g., `pages/15_hy_ig_spy`), the fallback silently routes to a non-existent path. Cloud raises `StreamlitPageNotFoundError`; local smoke never exercises `st.page_link`, so the bug is invisible locally.
- **The fix must be paired with the pair build step, not deferred.** When Ace creates page wrapper files (e.g., `15_hy_ig_spy_*.py`), the same commit must add the routing entry. Never split them.
- **`known_stress_episodes` contract between Evan and the probability panel had two incompatible forms.** The validator assumed `(start, end)` tuples; Evan's metadata used `{label, start, end, note}` dicts. The normaliser fix (`_to_window()`) accepts both, but the real fix is: schema must declare one canonical form. Add to data-agent SOP: `known_stress_episodes` must be a list of `{start, end}` dicts (extra keys OK). Validator should remain tolerant for legacy tuple form.
- **Cloud-only bugs that pass local smoke are almost always caused by:** (a) page routing mismatch (`st.page_link` not exercised by smoke), (b) stale getattr fallbacks (module cache), (c) missing artifact path (path built differently on Cloud). When cloud-verify reports a traceback that smoke did not catch, look for these three causes first.

## 2026-04-23 — `.get(key, default)` does NOT rescue `None` values

If a key is **present** with a `null`/`None` value, `.get(key, default)` returns `None`, not the default. The default only fires on missing key. So `float(winner.get("threshold_value", 0.5))` blows up when the JSON has `"threshold_value": null` (which is how legacy `winner_summary.json` files ship). Every `float(dict.get(..., <fallback>))` on producer-shared JSON must be wrapped in try/except `(TypeError, ValueError)` for defense-in-depth — especially in shared components consumed by pairs from multiple pipeline generations. Emit a visible `st.info` banner on fallback so schema drift is visible, not silent. Quincy's cloud-verify caught 6 regressions from exactly this pattern (Wave 10I.A).

## 2026-04-23 — Template changes require bypass audit

When adding a feature to a centralized render template (like `render_methodology_page`), hand-written pages that predate the template won't pick up the change. Always run `grep -L "<template_fn>" app/pages/*.py` after wiring. In this codebase, 5/8 methodology pages bypass the template; only 3 use it (`14_indpro_xlp`, `15_hy_ig_spy`, `9_hy_ig_v2_spy` — and the latter only partially: story/strategy yes, methodology NO). Silent cloud failure resulting from this took a full QA re-verify + 2 user-initiated cloud reboots to diagnose.

