# Memories: App Dev Ace

Specific incidents. Each entry is a concrete wave/commit/bug with the lesson at the end.

## Incidents

### Wave 3 (2026-04-19) -- Dot-Com zoom "chart pending" while canonical JSON existed

`app/components/charts.py::load_plotly_chart` was render-only -- it never returned the Figure. `_resolve_history_zoom_paths("history_zoom_dotcom", "hy_ig_v2_spy")` correctly returned the canonical path with `exists=True` (59 KB valid Plotly JSON, one trace, title present), but any silent parse / cache failure downstream had no observable exit signal at the call site -- so the GATE-25 "chart pending" placeholder rendered and the bug escaped review.

**Fix:** made `load_plotly_chart` return the Figure (or None on miss); wrapped the JSON parser in try/except with a visible `st.warning`; authored APP-ST1 (Loader End-to-End Smoke Test) that AST-parses every page and exec each call site.

**Lesson:** consumer contracts must return values; never side-effect-only. Artifact-existence checks are not rendering checks.

### Wave 4D-2 (2026-04-19) -- First production consumer-side schema integration

Integrated `app/components/schema_check.py` (`validate_or_die`, `validate_soft`, `SchemaValidationError`) as the consumer-side counterpart to Evan Wave 4D-1 producer migration. Retired the `_SIGNAL_CODE_TO_COLUMN` literal-name fallback map (40+ lines in `probability_engine_panel.py`) -- structurally unnecessary once the schema guarantees `signal_column`. First production invocation of APP-DIR1 (Evan <-> Dana direction check); result was agreement on `countercyclical`. Authored APP-WS1 / APP-SEV1 / APP-DIR1 SOP rules in the same wave.

**Lesson:** when a producer schema lands, the corresponding consumer fallback becomes structurally unnecessary. Retire it rather than leaving it as dead defensive code -- dead code hides real contract failures.

### Wave 5C (2026-04-19) -- Retro-apply of Wave-5B vocabulary rules to HY-IG v2

45 captions migrated to APP-CC1 canonical prefixes (`"What this shows:"` x20, `"Why this matters:"` x17, `"How to read it:"` x4, `"Caveat:"` x4). 17 expanders retitled per APP-EX1 (`"Plain English version"` -> `"Plain English"`; per-method deep-dive questions preserved as italicised first line of body). 22 `st.error`/`st.warning`/`st.info` sites got Plain English ELI5 blocks (was 0/22 before). Trigger-card sparklines rewritten to pull real crossings from `signals_20260410.parquet` -- HY-IG up-crossing located at 2010-04-06 (Eurozone stress), down-crossing at 2016-02-11 (oil/China scare recovery). Flat/HOLD scenario has no real instance (HMM is bimodal), fell back to stylised with transparent caption disclosure.

**Lesson:** retro-apply is mechanical once the registries exist. Do the schema/registry authoring first (Wave 5B-2), then the code migration (Wave 5C) is pure grep-and-replace.

### Wave 6B (2026-04-19) -- Loader refactor per META-AL

Dropped `output/_comparison/` fallback chain from `charts.py::_resolve_history_zoom_paths`. Post META-AL: canonical layer is the events_registry metadata (VIZ-V12), NOT rendered pixels. Every pair ships its own dual-panel zoom chart per VIZ-V1. Extended `chart_type_registry.json` with non-method rows (`hero`, `equity_curves`, `equity_drawdown`, `tournament_scatter`, `history_zoom_*`, `trade_log_preview`, `signal_timeseries`, `position_timeseries`, `regime_shading_backdrop`) per APP-CH1.

**Lesson:** "canonical" vs "override" layers should be metadata (registries, schemas) not rendered artifacts. A rendered chart is a consumable; the chart identity lives in the registry.

### Wave 7B (2026-04-19) -- New Methodology table renderers

Created `app/components/signal_universe_table.py` (150 lines) rendering `signal_scope.json` indicator and target axis tables; and `app/components/analyst_suggestions_table.py` (129 lines) rendering `analyst_suggestions.json` with the ECON-AS informational disclaimer. Both load via `validate_or_die`; both carry APP-CC1 `"What this shows:"` caption above and APP-SE5 1-line takeaway below; both META-ELI5 fallback on missing file. Extended `smoke_schema_consumers.py` to validate the two new producer artifacts (3 -> 5 cases).

**Lesson:** pair-agnostic by design (`pair_id` parameter) means Wave 7B components automatically work for any future pair once its `signal_scope.json` / `analyst_suggestions.json` ship. No per-pair branching.

### Wave 7C BLOCKER (2026-04-20) -- Prose leak in `.py` pages bypassed narrative authority

Quincy Wave 7C QA caught 5 prose citations in `app/pages/9_hy_ig_v2_spy_{story,methodology}.py` that contradicted Ray cleaned narrative: "We include the CCC-BB quality spread as one of our tournament signals (S5) precisely because..." -- but `signal_scope.json` had CCC-BB as off-scope and Ray narrative had already removed the claim. Fix: rewrote the 5 sites to point at `signal_scope.json` as authoritative and retitled the Story-page expander "Deeper dive (background only -- out of scope for this pair)". Filed BL-004 for cross-pair audit.

**Lesson:** inline prose in `.py` files bypasses the narrative editor authority. If Ray owns the narrative, Ace must render narrative markdown -- not write prose in Python.

### Wave 8B-2 (2026-04-20) -- Unit-form consumer fix (the "+0.1%" bug)

15 consumer sites updated per Evan Wave 8B-1 inventory. `f"+{val:.1f}%"` -> `f"{val:+.1%}"` on 7 format strings. Fallback literals: `11.33 -> 0.1133`, `-10.2 -> -0.102`, `-33.7 -> -0.337`. Tournament leaderboard (lines 761/762) kept its `NumberColumn(format="%.1f%%")` and scaled display-only `x100` on a copy -- preserves sorting affordances of NumberColumn. Chose boundary-normalization at `pair_registry.py`: `_dd_scale = 100.0 if pair_dir == "hy_ig_v2_spy" else 1.0` so `pair["max_drawdown"]` contract stays uniformly percent-form across all pairs (other pairs still percent-form per BL-002). `app.py:272-273` stays unchanged.

**Lesson:** smart boundaries beat propagating changes. One normalization at `pair_registry` read-site preserved a uniform consumer contract across 73 pairs. Propagating the ratio form up into `app.py` would have required changing 2 format strings + revalidating `_mdd_color` thresholds for every card.

### Wave 10D fix (2026-04-20) -- indpro_xlp structural divergence from reference template

All 4 `indpro_xlp` pages were missing `render_breadcrumb()` — built without consulting the reference template (`9_hy_ig_v2_spy_story.py`). Additionally, `14_indpro_xlp_evidence.py` used a flat 4-tab layout instead of the Level 1 / Level 2 + `render_method_block()` structure. Fixed: breadcrumb added to all 4 pages; evidence page rewritten as 3-block (Correlation, Granger Causality, Regime Analysis) with 8-element method blocks. Smoke test: `failures=0`.

**Lesson:** always derive new pair pages from `9_hy_ig_v2_spy_story.py` (and siblings), not from scratch. Even a well-intentioned from-scratch implementation will miss mandatory components. Template derivation is not optional.

### APP-SS1 (2026-04-20) -- Silent schema reader mismatch: legacy `in_scope.*` path vs current `indicator_axis.*` path

`14_indpro_xlp_methodology.py` read `scope.get("in_scope", {})` -> `in_scope.get("indicator_derivatives", [])`. The key `in_scope` does not exist in the current schema (migrated to `indicator_axis`/`target_axis`), so both derivative columns rendered silently empty — no Python error, no exception, just blank content.

**Fix:** Updated reader to `scope.get("indicator_axis", {}).get("derivatives", [])` with explicit empty-check and `st.error` guard per APP-SS1 reference pattern.

**Lesson:** silent schema reader mismatch (legacy `in_scope.*` path vs current `indicator_axis.*` path) produces empty Signal Universe with no Python error — always validate non-empty render at gate. `scope.get("missing_key", {})` returns `{}` silently; downstream `.get("missing_sub_key", [])` returns `[]` silently — the entire section appears blank with zero feedback.

### APP-PT1 (2026-04-20) -- Page-template abstraction (indpro_xlp migrated)

Structural drift from the reference pair (Wave 10D bugs: breadcrumb missing, Evidence tabs flat instead of Level-1/Level-2, Signal Universe empty, direction-check skipped) is a class of bug whose root cause is copy-paste page files. Authored APP-PT1 rule + `app/components/page_templates.py` (~850 LoC) with 4 template functions:
`render_story_page`, `render_evidence_page`, `render_strategy_page`, `render_methodology_page`. Created `app/pair_configs/__init__.py` + `indpro_xlp_config.py` with `STORY_CONFIG` / `EVIDENCE_METHOD_BLOCKS` / `STRATEGY_CONFIG` / `METHODOLOGY_CONFIG` (dataclass). Replaced the 4 `14_indpro_xlp_*.py` pages with thin wrappers (6-11 lines each; zero `st.*` calls verified via AST). Color palette centralised as `PALETTE` dict in the template module.

Extended `smoke_loader.py` to scan the new `page_templates.py` + `{pair_id}_config.py` for `load_plotly_chart` literal args and `_CHART_NAME` assignments, since thin-wrapper pages no longer contain chart calls. indpro_xlp smoke: 8 PASS / 0 FAIL. Regression checks: hy_ig_v2_spy 15/0, umcsent_xlv 7/0.

Schema-consumer smoke: APP-WS1 / DATA-D6 / ECON-UD / ECON-AS / APP-DIR1 all PASS on indpro_xlp (5/5). Direction triangulation 2-way: Evan=countercyclical, Dana=countercyclical.

**Lesson:** structural drift is a class of bug fixed at the template layer, not per-page. Thin wrappers enforce consistency by construction — the page file has no room to diverge because it has no `st.*` calls. This is the Wave 10D root-cause fix, not a retro-patch of individual pages.

**Lesson:** when page files stop containing chart literals (thin wrappers), the smoke test must follow the chart literals to wherever they moved (template + pair config). AST-scanning multiple source files is cheap; assuming one file location is fragile.

